speechtext:
Here is the resolution passed
unanimously two weeks ago by the British Columbia legislature:
Whereas this legislature at its last session unanimously passed a resolution urging adoption of an all-Canadian route through the Yellowknife pass for the proposed transmission of natural gas from the gas fields of Alberta to Vancouver and ether British Columbia points;
A year ago that resolution was passed stating that the gas route should come from Edmonton through the Yellowhead pass.
And whereas important gas discoveries have since been made in the Peace river area of Alberta and British Columbia, which gas can best be marketed via a route through the Pine pass;
And whereas the Alberta Petroleum and Natural Gas Conservation Board has recommended in its interim report of January, 1951 (1) that the only gas available for export is the gas lying beyond economic reach of Alberta consumers, which includes the gas reserves of the Peace river area, and (2) that no gas found in the major gas fields lying

Border Pipeline Corporation in that part of the province from the Edmonton area south to the international border could be allocated to a market outside of the province;
Hon. members who served on the standing committee on railways, canals and telegraph lines will realize that only recently an exception has been made to that rule whereby provision is made to pipe some gas into Montana for war purposes, but only on a temporary basis for a period of five years. Then the resolution goes on:
And whereas the interests of Canada as a whole will best be served if natural gas from the Peace river areas of Alberta and British Columbia is transmitted to its closest markets, namely, British Columbia and the Pacific coast;
And whereas it is vital that the maximum encouragement be given to the discovery and development of the oil and gas resources of this province and to the industrial development of the interior of the province;
And whereas, in view of the foregoing facts, the route which best serves the interests of this province and the dominion as a whole is the aforesaid Pine pass route and such pipe line route having been proposed;
The Westcoast Transmission Company have now switched their gas route from the Yellow-head to the Pine pass route because of the finding of the Alberta petroleum and natural gas conservation board.
And whereas any proposed crude oil pipe line to the Pacific coast should, in the interests of the province, follow an all-Canadian route, and at the same time provide for the marketing of oil found in this province;
And whereas it is proposed to build a crude oil pipe line from northern Alberta to Vancouver via the Yellowhead and Pine routes to provide for marketing such oil.
That is, there would be a main line from . Edmonton and a branch line from the Peace river down through the Pine pass. The resolution continues:
Therefore be it resolved, that this legislative assembly urges upon the appropriate authorities, in the interests of both British Columbia and Canada, the adoption of (1) the Pine pass route for natural gas transmission pipe line and (2) the combined Yellowhead and Pine pass route for the crude oil pipe line, which will provide for the marketing of oil discovered in British Columbia.
That resolution sets out the picture at the present time in Canada's western province and I contend that it should be supported by every hon. member from our province, no matter what his political views may be. The Border Pipeline Company advocates a policy which is just the reverse.
Now is the time for foundation laying. It is not too late to work out the best policy and to follow it. As members of this present parliament we have it in our power to see that a proper policy is followed. Oil being sent to the east is being transported through the United States and we cannot do anything about that now. It may be the policy of the oil

interests to send oil to the west through the United States also just as they have done with the oil to the east. Hon. members need not be surprised if they find the oil interests issuing statements or propaganda that this oil should go down through the United States; that the best thing for Canada would be to have it go through the United States. We should not forget that they are not controlled in Canada. The oil interests are not working for Canada, they are working for their own profits. That should be remembered at all times when national issues are being decided. If more money is to be received by building this oil pipe line directly to the United States it means that much more money for the oil companies. They may well advocate that oil should be brought to the largest market, which undoubtedly is in the states of Washington and Oregon rather than British Columbia, by the shortest possible route, regardless of national interests. They will plead economic reasons and all that sort of thing, but let the members of the house beware.
I hope that the house will make sure that oil being sent to the Pacific coast goes by a Canada-first route. The picture is very well summed up in an article in the Saturday Night of June 20, 1950, entitled, "Pipe Vancouver the gas-but how?" That is not spelled "Howe". This is an article by Michael Barkway, one of the distinguished members of our press gallery, who sums up the question neatly in these words:
It poses the question that has faced Canada throughout her history; shall we let the forces of geography work out our destiny, or shall we bend them to our national designs?
I suggest to hon. members that it is in the national interest of Canada that this charter should not be granted and that no charter should be granted to any company which proposes to build its main line to the Pacific coast through the United States.
